    "result_text": "The build passes. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed the `customerCode` format from `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`.\n\n**Code change** \u2014 `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`:\n- `CITY3` = upper-cased first three letters of the owner's city\n- `LAST3` = upper-cased first three letters of lastName (unchanged)\n- `NNNN` = now a **per-city** 4-digit sequence \u2014 one more than the number of owners already in that city (`findAll()` filtered by city, matching the existing `AssignOwnerHousehold`/`CountOwnerNamesakes` patterns), rather than the old global count.\n\n**Derived values rebuilt on the new format:**\n- `membershipNumber` (`AssignOwnerMembershipNumber.java`) is built as `getCustomerCode() + \"-M\" + YY`, so it picks up the new customerCode automatically. I updated its stale javadoc example.\n\n**Docs updated** to reflect the new format: the javadoc on both function classes and the `customerCode`/`membershipNumber` schema descriptions and examples in `openapi.yml` (`SYD-SMI-0007` / `SYD-SMI-0007-M26`). The DB column widths (`customer_code VARCHAR(20)`, `membership_number VARCHAR(30)`) comfortably fit the longer values.\n\n**Tests:** The full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the acceptance test `coreCustomerCodeHasCityPrefix`, which asserts the `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}` format.",
